00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:12.289
The commercial airline industry is the classic
duopoly.

00:00:12.289 --> 00:00:17.870
In the US there’s Boeing—the long successful
manufacturer most known for its iconic 747—and

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:22.010
in Europe there’s Airbus—the more recent
entrant formed as a consortium of various

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:24.980
European manufacturers—and that’s pretty
much it.

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:31.120
2/3 of all airplanes currently flying commercially
are made by one of these two manufacturers.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:35.060
But there’s something these manufacturers
don’t do—they don’t make small planes,

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:36.940
but that’s not for lack of trying.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:42.060
Airbus tried when they made the a318—a variant
of their successful a320—but only sold 80

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.060
of them.

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:49.239
Boeing did as well with their 717 but it too
was a commercial failure with only 156 sales.

00:00:49.239 --> 00:00:52.329
But that doesn’t mean that there’s no
place for small planes.

00:00:52.329 --> 00:00:56.920
In fact, it’s a significant market that
the giants of the aviation industry have not

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:58.290
been able to touch.

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:02.149
If you step on a plane to a small airport
nowadays, there’s a good chance that it’s

00:01:02.149 --> 00:01:04.519
either made by Embraer or Bombardier.

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:09.500
Embraer, the Brazilian manufacturer, launched
their E-Jet family just over 15 years ago

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:12.970
and to date have sold nearly 1,500 of them.

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:17.790
By all accounts its a fantastic airplane and
there are whole airports that rely on them.

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:22.840
Nearly 70% of the more than a hundred daily
flights out of the small London City Airport

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:24.700
operate using an Embraer E-Jet.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:29.220
Due to the noise restrictions, steep approach
angle, and short runway this is one of the

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:33.560
few planes that can operate there and some
airlines, such as British Airways, own the

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:36.920
E-Jet exclusively for use at this airport.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:42.869
The US, with its nearly 400 commercial airports
and vast size, has service to many more smaller

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:48.280
airports than Europe or Asia thanks to government
subsidies so hundreds of airports in the US

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:50.890
are served by non-Boeing or Airbus jets.

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:55.830
There’s a serious place for small planes,
and Bombardier knows this.

00:01:55.830 --> 00:02:00.310
Bombardier, while still one of Canada’s
largest companies, is a small player in the

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:04.810
commercial airplane market only making about
6% of the world’s commercial planes.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:09.840
The company has long made the successful CRJ
regional jets, but ten years ago Bombardier

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.010
had an idea for something else.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:16.650
They were going to make a larger, more efficient
airplane to fit between the size of small

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:21.310
regional jets and the larger planes of Boeing
and Airbus—the C Series.

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:26.260
This plane was going to take advantage of
all the modern advancements in aircraft design—composite

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:31.130
construction, advanced aerodynamics, and high-bypass
engines—to make one of the most efficient

00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:33.190
small airplanes on the market.

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:38.450
From 2007 onwards the parts were selected,
the plane was developed, and sales were booked.

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:43.580
Then, on September 16th, 2013, it took flight
for the first time.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:47.810
At this point the company was adamant that
the aircraft would enter into commercial service

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:50.590
just one year later in 2014.

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:52.569
But then the problems began.

00:02:52.569 --> 00:02:56.910
While doing routing testing on the ground
at the Mirabel airport manufacturing base,

00:02:56.910 --> 00:03:00.790
one of the test aircraft suffered an uncontained
engine failure.

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:04.890
Not only did this strike a serious blow to
the aircraft development timeline, it also

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:09.680
prevented the manufacturer from exhibiting
their new plane at the Farnborough Airshow—the

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:13.890
largest and most important trade-show in the
industry where the company expected to secure

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:15.430
crucial aircraft orders.

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:20.660
The show only happens every two years so this
was a significant setback.

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:25.390
Some questioned whether the program could
survive this blow, and it only barely did.

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:28.659
The program was nearly out of money in 2015.

00:03:28.659 --> 00:03:33.110
Without the significant financial support
of the governments of Canada and Quebec, the

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:39.159
program and company likely would not have
survived, but it did, and on June 15th, 2016,

00:03:39.159 --> 00:03:44.230
the aircraft entered service with Swiss Airlines
on its first ever commercial flight from Zurich

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:45.349
to Paris.

00:03:45.349 --> 00:03:48.820
By all accounts the C Series is a fantastic
airplane.

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:53.030
For airlines it’s efficient and versatile
while passengers praise its rare level of

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:54.980
passenger comfort for a small plane.

00:03:54.980 --> 00:04:01.120
It even has a range of up to 3,800 miles meaning
it could hypothetically fly transatlantic

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:04.940
into small airports like London and Belfast
City Airports.

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:11.319
As of October 2017 there are 14 C series planes
currently flying—9 for Swiss Airlines and

00:04:11.319 --> 00:04:17.560
5 for Air Baltic—but they have nearly 350
orders—enough to make the program profitable—but

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:22.490
until recently, they lacked the most valuable
kind of order—American ones.

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:27.779
With its tendency to use smaller planes, the
American market will make or break the program,

00:04:27.779 --> 00:04:29.619
and Bombardier knew this.

00:04:29.619 --> 00:04:33.860
They were desperate for an order from one
of the main three US airlines to prove its

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:40.289
viability, so much so that they allegedly
sold 75 of them to Delta for only $20 million

00:04:40.289 --> 00:04:44.460
dollars each—a quarter of the $80 million
list price.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:49.759
That was an amazing price for Delta, but it
was low enough that it might have been illegal.

00:04:49.759 --> 00:04:51.910
This is a practice known as dumping.

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:57.270
The cost for Bombardier to make a C Series
plane is about $30 million so it was selling

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:01.580
these planes that they hadn’t even made
yet at a loss because they wanted to gain

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:03.610
prevalence in the US market.

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:08.789
Predatory pricing techniques like this are
against the law in the US and many other countries,

00:05:08.789 --> 00:05:13.699
so Boeing took action and filed a dumping
petition with the US department of commerce.

00:05:13.699 --> 00:05:17.740
But here’s the problem—Boeing doesn’t
make an equivalent airplane to the C Series.

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:23.789
They say that the dumping will curtail the
sale of its smaller 737-700 planes, but Delta

00:05:23.789 --> 00:05:27.220
themselves has said that they didn’t want
the 737.

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:33.259
Boeing couldn’t offer them a delivery slot
before 2020 and, the 737 is a fundamentally

00:05:33.259 --> 00:05:34.259
different plane.

00:05:34.259 --> 00:05:38.939
The C Series has better range, can take-off
from a shorter runway, is more efficient,

00:05:38.939 --> 00:05:41.919
and is empirically a smaller airplane.

00:05:41.919 --> 00:05:46.719
Boeing claims that Bombardier received subsidies
that allowed for the below-market pricing,

00:05:46.719 --> 00:05:51.089
but the money Bombardier received from the
governments of Quebec and Canada came in either

00:05:51.089 --> 00:05:55.169
exchange for ownership of the program or in
the form of interest-free loans that will

00:05:55.169 --> 00:05:56.330
be paid back.

00:05:56.330 --> 00:06:02.460
Boeing, on the other hand, has received over
$14 billion in subsidies in the last 20 years

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:05.860
through tax breaks from US state and federal
governments.

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:10.610
Nonetheless, Boeing asked the US Department
of Commerce to impose an 80% tariff on the

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:13.690
plane to bring the price above the manufacturing
cost.

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:18.999
The US Department of Commerce responded by
placing a 300% tariff on the plane.

00:06:18.999 --> 00:06:22.479
At this point it seemed like the end of the
line for the Bombardier plane.

00:06:22.479 --> 00:06:27.330
With a tariff that high, it could never be
both competitive and profitable in the crucial

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:28.869
US market.

00:06:28.869 --> 00:06:30.210
But then Airbus stepped in.

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:37.169
On October 16th, 2017 the two companies announced
that Airbus would be acquiring a 50.01% stake

00:06:37.169 --> 00:06:38.689
in the C Series program.

00:06:38.689 --> 00:06:42.990
Bombardier is just giving away this stake
for free, but the reason this is so great

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:47.860
for them is, for one, Airbus is a marketing
and sales powerhouse.

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:52.179
Their resources far outstrip those of Bombardier
and they will be able to sell more planes

00:06:52.179 --> 00:06:54.599
than Bombardier could ever have dreamt of.

00:06:54.599 --> 00:07:00.149
On the other side, since the C Series doesn’t
really overlap in size with any Airbus plane,

00:07:00.149 --> 00:07:04.909
Airbus gains a new, modern airplane in their
line up that will allow airlines to have truly

00:07:04.909 --> 00:07:06.339
all-Airbus fleets.

00:07:06.339 --> 00:07:11.369
But the real reason Bombardier partnered up
with Airbus is because of this—Airbus’

00:07:11.369 --> 00:07:13.789
final assembly line in Mobile, Alabama.

00:07:13.789 --> 00:07:18.520
By assembling the C Series aircraft in the
US, they’ll essentially be American planes

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:23.759
which means that they won’t be subject to
US import tariffs, at least according to Bombardier.

00:07:23.759 --> 00:07:26.749
Boeing believes that that they will still
be subject to the tariff since they’re still

00:07:26.749 --> 00:07:31.149
the same planes that received subsidies, while
some external observers have suggested that

00:07:31.149 --> 00:07:36.559
only the 48% of the plane manufactured outside
of the US will be subject to the 300% tariff.

00:07:36.559 --> 00:07:40.479
What will actually happen is up to the US
Department of Commerce.

00:07:40.479 --> 00:07:44.639
Independent analysts have estimated that over
the next 20 years, there will be demand for

00:07:44.639 --> 00:07:49.149
about 5,500 aircraft of the C Series’ size.

00:07:49.149 --> 00:07:54.080
Before the Airbus takeover, Bombardier was
expected to fulfill 40% of that demand—just

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:55.460
over 2,000 planes.

00:07:55.460 --> 00:08:01.589
After the takeover, the C Series is expected
to capture up to 60% of that market—well

00:08:01.589 --> 00:08:03.169
over 3,000 planes.

00:08:03.169 --> 00:08:08.509
That’s tens of billions of dollars in additional
revenue and the C Series now has real potential

00:08:08.509 --> 00:08:09.909
to become an iconic airplane.

00:08:09.909 --> 00:08:10.909
Bombardier and Airbus are the big winners
with this outcome.

00:08:10.909 --> 00:08:15.059
While it would have been hard to predict this
turn of events, this takeover, directly brought

00:08:15.059 --> 00:08:19.249
on by Boeing’s actions, proves a hard-hitting
blow to the company.

00:08:19.249 --> 00:08:24.270
Without spending a cent on development, Airbus
now has a plane in a market segment that Boeing

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:25.430
cannot compete with.

00:08:25.430 --> 00:08:30.929
The C Series will dominate this market segment
and every cent Airbus and Bombardier makes

00:08:30.929 --> 00:08:33.159
is money lost on Boeing’s part.

00:08:33.159 --> 00:08:38.349
Just as Boeing expected, the C Series will
hurt its business in the US but now it will

00:08:38.349 --> 00:08:41.579
happen with a fully-legal, American made plane.

